"""Retest section of the results report (23 October 2026). Inserted at the top of the report Doc, before the first run.
Styles as report_text.py. Placeholders are filled when the H6 synthesis is final."""
R = []
def T(x): R.append(('TITLE', x))
def H2(x): R.append(('HEADING_2', x))
def H3(x): R.append(('HEADING_3', x))
def P(x): R.append(('NORMAL_TEXT', x))
def B(x): R.append(('BULLET', x))

T("Narrative Selection and Societal Endurance: Results by Hypothesis")
P("Retest of 23 October 2026, followed by the first run of 16 to 23 October 2026. Companion to the protocol: Section 12 logs the deviations (D24 onward for the retest) and Section 16 gives the retest results. Author: Hamza Bassatné. Data collection, coding and analysis were carried out with the assistance of Claude (Anthropic).")

H2("Part 1. The retest (23 October 2026)")
H3("What changed and why")
P("The first run measured narrative content by counting moral words in 15 canonical texts, so every polity of one religion got the same score and there were only 15 independent values. The retest, approved by the author on 23 October 2026, replaces that measure everywhere. Content in Study 1 is now the Seshat Equinox ideology index (EPI): historians' codes of whether a polity's normative ideology treats people as equals and holds elites and rulers to the same norms as commoners. It varies polity by polity, covers polities without scriptures, and involves no AI coder. Covariates are measured century by century and each region is compared with its own past (region fixed effects). H4b was replaced by H4b* (recovery from crisis), H5 became a head-to-head test, H6 was tested on published experiments, H7 was restated with an equivalence rule, and H8, H9a, H9b and Study 2 were strengthened. An optional H10 asks whether the stories themselves are selected.")
P("All results remain exploratory. Hazard ratios (HR) below 1 mean longer survival; odds ratios (OR) below 1 mean lower odds. Intervals are 95 percent unless marked 90 percent. Study 1 p-values are Holm-corrected across H1, H2, H3, H4a, H4b*, H5 and H7.")

H3("Summary of the retest")
B("H1, ideology content predicts endurance: inconclusive. HR 1.03 per SD [0.72, 1.47] in 221 polities with 117 endings, each region compared with its own past. Within the same religion: 0.94 [0.71, 1.26]. Neither a protective effect nor its absence is established.")
B("H2, robust to the drivers of power, century by century: inconclusive. With agriculture, military technology, population, territory, capital size, social complexity and writing, all lagged and varying within polities where Seshat dates them: HR 1.18 [0.77, 1.81].")
B("H3, content precedes complexity: inconclusive. Ideology does not predict the next century's complexity (b = -0.008 SD [-0.047, 0.032]), and complexity does not predict the next century's ideology. Complexity jumps in the same century that a more egalitarian ideology arrives, which fits ideology and complexity coming together with new polities, not one leading the other.")
B("H4a, content prevents internal collapse rather than conquest: inconclusive in the primary within-region model (internal-collapse HR 1.11 [0.51, 2.44]). In the between-region competing-risks model, higher EPI goes with less internal collapse (sub-hazard ratio 0.78 [0.61, 0.99]) and more conquest (1.41 [1.14, 1.74]).")
B("H4b*, content helps polities recover from crises: inconclusive. Survival of 109 crises: OR 1.12 [0.74, 1.71]; no effect on severity or reform.")
B("H5, the message rather than the vessel: estimate only, because H2 found no content effect. Moralizing-religion codes add nothing (joint p = 0.46).")
B("H6, cooperative stories raise cooperation with strangers: inconclusive. Of 24 eligible randomized experiments, only 5 could be read and converted to an effect size; all involve children or adolescents and modern stories, and none was preregistered. The pooled effect is large (g = 0.74 [0.09, 1.39]), but heterogeneity is large too, the bias-corrected estimate does not exclude 0, and the decision rule requires a positive preregistered estimate, which cannot exist here.")
B("H7, the same effect in every era: not falsified, not shown. Era estimates 0.99, 1.22 and 1.18 with wide intervals; no interaction (p = 0.84); the equivalence rule is not met.")
B("H8, service-legitimated rule outlasts force: partly supported. Modern autocracies (V-Dem) that ruled more cooperatively ended violently far less often, even measured 10 or 20 years before the end (OR 0.65 [0.48, 0.87] at 10 years), and their leaders far less often left office irregularly (11 versus 51 percent) or ended in exile, prison or death (15 versus 54 percent). But within the same country the link disappears (OR 0.91 [0.59, 1.40]), and in premodern polities executive constraint predicts nothing.")
B("H9a, the seven cooperative behaviours are judged good everywhere: falsified for respect for property, narrowly, by the pre-set rule; holds for the other six. In 53 societies from 42 language families, 844 of 869 stated judgements approve the behaviour. Property is condemned in a family-weighted 5.2 percent of judgements [0.8, 10.7], just over the 5 percent threshold, and 6 of the 8 condemnations concern the property of enemies or of landlords: own-group property is condemned in 1 of 91 judgements.")
B("H9b, belief in a moralizing God spreads by descent and scale: descent supported, scale on trees not shown. Moralizing gods cluster on all three dated language trees (D = 0.05 to 0.29, where 1 is random), but the correlated-evolution test finds no sign that they are gained more readily under political hierarchy (LR 7.98, df 12, p = 0.79).")
B("Study 2, folklore content and trust: inconclusive. Coding what 2,436 motifs reward and punish gives a score that does not predict trust across countries (0.02 SD per SD [-0.12, 0.16], 90 percent) or among children of immigrants (0.014 SD [-0.005, 0.034], 90 percent). The trickster-punishment measure, the positive control, still predicts trust (0.28 SD across countries).")
B("H10, the stories themselves are selected: inconclusive. Motifs that reward cooperation are not more widespread (they span slightly fewer continents, IRR 0.93 [0.87, 0.99]) and are not lost more slowly on the dated trees (p = 0.33).")

H3("H1: ideology content predicts endurance")
P("Claim. Polities whose normative ideology extends moral standing more widely last longer.")
P("Test. 221 polities with EPI and a known ending (117 endings: 42 internal collapses, 70 conquests), in 34 regions. EPI averages three Equinox items coded by Seshat's historians (ideological reinforcement of equality; ideology equates elites and commoners; ideology equates rulers and commoners), with unknowns treated as missing. A discrete-time hazard model on polity-centuries with region fixed effects, polity-age and era terms; p-values from a wild cluster score bootstrap over regions (9,999 draws), because there are only 30 to 34 regions. 29 polities needed new termination codes; blind AI coders produced them under the same codebook, and on the 6 coded twice they agreed on the type of ending in all 6.")
P("Result. HR 1.03 [0.72, 1.47] per SD of EPI (90 percent: 0.76 to 1.39; bootstrap p = 0.87). Comparing polities of the same religion: 0.94 [0.71, 1.26]. Without region fixed effects: 1.18 [0.90, 1.56] on all 34 regions; restricted to the 30 regions that have an ending, as in the main model, 1.31 [1.01, 1.71] (independent verification). A Cox model stratified by region: 1.25 [0.95, 1.64]. So comparisons between regions lean toward shorter lives for more egalitarian polities, the opposite of the claim, and comparisons within regions show nothing. Decision: inconclusive. The 90 percent interval is not inside the equivalence band (0.90 to 1.11), so H1 is not falsified either.")
P("Why. The sample is now large and the measure is polity-specific, yet the estimate sits on 1 with a wide interval. Two features limit it. EPI is mostly 0 or 1 (mean 0.42, SD 0.41), so it separates broad types of ideology rather than degrees. And the historians who coded ideology knew how each polity ended, which could tilt codes either way. What can be said is that a strong protective effect (HR below about 0.75 per SD) is unlikely.")

H3("H2: robust to the drivers of power, century by century")
P("Test. As H1, with controls measured one century earlier: agricultural productivity (Seshat Agri), military technology with iron and cavalry, log population, log territory, capital size, social complexity (first principal component of eight Seshat measures, 82 percent of their variance) and writing. Values are sampled with Seshat's own rules, 20 times, with imputation of gaps. Values change within a polity where Seshat dates them: population in 36 percent of polities, territory in 56 percent (with Cliopatria's year-by-year borders), complexity in 70 percent. So the comparison is within regions and, where the data allow, within polities over time, as the author asked.")
P("Result. HR 1.18 [0.77, 1.81]; bootstrap p = 0.46. Decision: inconclusive. A numerical failure in one of the 20 draws first produced an infinite interval; it was fixed by refitting with a second optimizer, which was needed in 3 of 180 fits (Deviation D36).")

H3("H3: content precedes complexity")
P("Test. About 384 region-centuries in 34 regions. Cross-lagged models with region fixed effects; Turchin and colleagues' model of complexity growth with EPI added; and an event study around the first century in which a region's polity reaches EPI of at least 2/3 (10 regions).")
P("Result. Forward lag (EPI to next-century complexity): -0.008 SD [-0.047, 0.032]. Reverse lag: -0.004 SD [-0.120, 0.111]. Decision: inconclusive. In the event study, complexity is flat before the switch (0.09 to 0.16 SD, not distinguishable from 0) and is 0.4 to 0.7 SD higher in the switch century and in four of the five centuries after it.")
P("Why. Ideology changes mostly when one polity succeeds another, so the century of the switch is also the century a new and usually larger polity arrives. Content and complexity rise together. Nothing here shows content leading.")

H3("H4a: internal collapse rather than conquest")
P("Result. Within regions: internal collapse HR 1.11 [0.51, 2.44] on 42 events; conquest 1.31 [0.71, 2.40]. Decision: inconclusive. Between regions (Fine-Gray, one row per polity, controls averaged over its life): internal-collapse sub-hazard 0.78 [0.61, 0.99], conquest sub-hazard 1.41 [1.14, 1.74]. The H8 test of the premodern ruler-equality item shows the same pattern (0.68 [0.47, 1.00]).")
P("Why. The between-region pattern is suggestive: polities with more egalitarian ideology seem to fall apart from within less often but to be conquered more often. Because it disappears within regions, it may reflect which regions had such ideologies (for example, regions later absorbed by large empires) rather than the ideology itself.")

H3("H4b*: recovery from crisis")
P("Test. 109 CrisisDB crises in polities with EPI. Outcomes: the polity survives 50 years after onset; severity (civil war, extermination, collapse, fragmentation); reform (new public goods or constitution); ruler removed.")
P("Result. Survival OR 1.12 [0.74, 1.71] (90 percent: 0.79 to 1.60, not inside the 0.80 to 1.25 band); severity ratio 1.04 [0.88, 1.24]; reform OR 0.99 [0.62, 1.56]; ruler removed OR 1.40 [0.88, 2.22]. Decision: inconclusive.")

H3("H5: the message rather than the vessel")
P("Result. With moralizing supernatural punishment, moralizing high gods and 'moral concern is primary' in the model, EPI's HR is 1.22 [0.80, 1.87]. The three vessel codes jointly add nothing (p = 0.46). Since H2 found no content effect, there is nothing for the vessel to absorb, and H5 is an estimate only.")

H3("H6: cooperative stories and cooperation with strangers")
P("Claim. Random exposure to a story whose content shows or endorses cooperation raises incentivized cooperation with anonymous others, compared with a neutral or matched text.")
P("Test. A systematic review with a protocol fixed on 23 October 2026 before any search: 12 query strings with follow-ups, the reference lists of six reviews and meta-analyses, forward citations of five key studies (about 500 citing works, through OpenAlex), and OSF and PsyArXiv. 148 records were recorded at screening: 24 eligible for the primary set (randomized, narrative treatment, incentivized giving or cooperation), 26 non-narrative moral messages, 19 religious primes, 13 narratives with non-incentivized helping, 11 honesty tasks, 1 transmission-chain study, and 54 excluded. Two independent AI extractors worked on every primary study and agreed on every number; a separate verifier re-checked a random 20 percent against the papers and found no numerical error. Effect sizes were computed by a frozen script (Hedges' g, random effects with robust variance, PET-PEESE).")
P("Result. Only 5 of the 24 primary studies yield an effect size. Fourteen could not be read (paywalled, blocked or failed to load), one is an ongoing trial with no results, and four report statistics that cannot be converted: no group sizes (Schwitzgebel et al.), regressions only within religious subgroups (Rand et al.), a p-value only (Horton et al.), or an interaction whose direction the paper never states (Sakai et al., where both groups stayed stable). The five usable effects: prosocial cartoons and donations by Chinese preschoolers (Zhang et al. 2021, g = 1.24); picture-book reading and sticker sharing in China (Chen et al. 2025, 0.08); stories about feelings and sticker generosity among children in Amman (Decety et al., 0.57); story-based training and dictator-game giving in Italy (Lombardi et al. 2021, 0.44); and prosocial short videos and real charity donations by Shanghai adolescents (Li and Li 2024, 1.26). Pooled g = 0.74 [0.09, 1.39] (robust variance, 4 degrees of freedom, p = 0.03), with large heterogeneity (tau squared 0.23). The bias-corrected PEESE estimate is 1.48 with a 95 percent interval of -0.13 to 3.08. No study was preregistered, so the preregistered-only estimate does not exist, and the selection model needs at least 8 effects. Decision: inconclusive.")
P("Why. What can be read of this literature is small and narrow: five studies, all with children or adolescents, all with modern stories or videos, none preregistered, none with traditional or religious narratives. The two largest effects come from studies with reporting problems (an F from a three-way ANOVA; tables and text that disagree). The PEESE correction moves the estimate up, not down, because the largest effects come from the larger studies; with five studies it is not informative. The only study of adults hearing traditional-style stories, Smith, Mabulla and Apicella (2023) with Hadza foragers, reports 0.22 more meat sticks given (90 percent interval -0.12 to 0.57) in its abstract, but its full text could not be read. The non-primary strata were screened but not extracted, so they are reported as counts only. H6 as specified (traditional narratives matched within a tradition) is untested; a new preregistered experiment remains the way to test it.")

H3("H7: the same effect in every era")
P("Claim, restated. The relation between moral content and endurance is the same before 500 BCE, from 500 BCE to 1500 CE, and after 1500 CE. It presupposes an effect and is a claim of no difference, so it needs an equivalence test.")
P("Result. Era HRs: 0.99 [0.51, 1.92] (18 endings), 1.22 [0.73, 2.01] (66), 1.18 [0.52, 2.72] (33). Interaction p = 0.84. The 90 percent intervals of the between-era ratios run from about 0.37 to 2.23, far outside the 0.67 to 1.5 band. Decision: not falsified (no significant reversal), not shown.")

H3("H8: service-legitimated rule outlasts force")
P("Part A, premodern polities (Seshat). Executive constraint (by government, by non-government actors, impeachment), 87 polities: no association with any ending (all endings HR 1.11, internal collapse 0.93), with crises, or with irregular successions (OR 1.02). Ideology equating rulers and commoners, 215 polities: internal collapse leans lower (sub-hazard 0.68 [0.47, 1.00]), but ruler changes were more often irregular (58 versus 45 percent, OR 1.24 [1.03, 1.50]) and tenures shorter. Estimates only.")
P("Part B, hindsight (V-Dem v15, autocracies 1789 to 2025). With the strategy score measured only from years at least 10 years before a regime's end, the most cooperative quarter ended violently 30 percent of the time against 53 percent for the least cooperative (OR 0.65 [0.48, 0.87], 373 regimes); with a 20-year lag, 17 against 55 percent (OR 0.42 [0.25, 0.71]). So hindsight in the expert codes does not explain the link. But comparing each country with itself, the link vanishes: conditional logit OR 0.91 [0.59, 1.40] at 10 years, 0.91 [0.74, 1.13] unlagged.")
P("Part C, leaders (Archigos 4.1, 2,020 leaders in 164 countries, 1875 to 2015). Leaders whose first three years were more cooperative left office irregularly less often (11 versus 51 percent between the top and bottom quarters; OR 0.53 [0.44, 0.64]), were removed by foreign force less often (OR 0.37), and less often ended in exile, prison or death (15 versus 54 percent; OR 0.58 [0.48, 0.69]), with income, population, tenure, decade and region held constant. Part D (assassination attempts) was not run; the data were not obtained.")
P("Decision. The primary component, violent ends with lagged exposure, holds across countries. Why the caveat matters: countries that are ruled cooperatively may differ in stable ways (wealth, institutions, geography) that also make violent ends rare, and the within-country test cannot separate cooperative rule from those traits. The leader-level result is the strongest new evidence, because it measures conduct at the start of each tenure and the outcome at its end.")

H3("H9a: the seven cooperative behaviours are judged good everywhere")
P("Test. 53 societies in 42 language families from ethnographies published before 1929 (the 30 of the first run, 16 further verified candidates and 7 from a new search; the search for Uralic, Tungusic and Caucasian peoples found none, and only the Ottoman Turks for Turkic). 5,172 paragraphs containing both a behaviour term and an evaluation term, up to 30 per behaviour per society. Blind AI coders recorded whether each paragraph states how the society judges the behaviour, the judgement, and the target of the act. A random 20 percent (1,034) was coded twice: Krippendorff alpha 0.84 for relevance and 0.83 for valence (0.92 when both coders found the passage relevant). Every negative judgement was coded three times: 25 of the first coder's 26 were confirmed. On the random sample, the two coders agreed on 4 of 6 negative judgements, closing the first run's untested gap.")
P("Result. 844 of 869 judgements approve. Family-weighted share condemning: group loyalty 0 percent (154 judgements), bravery 0.5 percent (141), deference 3.2 percent (121), reciprocity 3.4 percent (81), helping kin 3.5 percent (175), fair division 3.7 percent (18), respect for property 5.2 percent (179) [0.8, 10.7]. Every behaviour except fair division reaches the 73 judgements the plan set as the minimum for a precise estimate. Decision: falsified for respect for property; not falsified for the other six; the 95 percent upper bound is below 5 percent for group loyalty and bravery.")
P("Why property fails. The target coding shows where approval stops. Own-group property: 1 condemnation in 91 judgements; kin: 0 in 12; strangers: 0 in 9. Enemy property: 4 of 6 judgements praise taking it (Rwala and Kiowa raid boasts). The rest are Mongol admiration for clever thieves and Russian peasants' view that landlords' land belongs to those who work it. The unweighted share is 4.5 percent, below the line; family weighting tips it over. So the rule as written is broken, but the pattern is the one the theory's in-group version predicts: respect for property is universal inside the group and stops at its edge.")
P("Limits. Coders split on whether blood revenge counts as reciprocity; without the 17 revenge judgements the reciprocity share is 4.7 percent (post hoc check). The review coders saw batches made up only of first-coder negatives, which may have favoured confirmation. Fair division has only 18 judgements.")

H3("H9b: moralizing gods spread by descent and scale")
P("Test. Dated trees from D-PLACE: Austronesian (47 Ethnographic Atlas societies), Bantu (63) and Indo-European (31). The D statistic for clustering, and Pagel's test of correlated evolution between a moralizing high god and political hierarchy, fitted by maximum likelihood with code checked on simulated data.")
P("Result. Moralizing gods cluster strongly on all three trees (D = 0.09, 0.29 and 0.05; 0 is Brownian clustering and 1 random), while hierarchy does not (D = 0.83 to 1.03). The correlated-evolution test finds no dependence (pooled LR 7.98, df 12, p = 0.79). Decision: descent supported; the scale part is not shown on the trees. Prevalence is low on two trees (13 and 8 percent) and near-universal on the third (87 percent), so the dependence test has little to work with.")

H3("Study 2: folklore content and trust")
P("Test. 2,436 of Berezkin's motifs coded for what they reward and punish across the seven behaviours (35 percent show an act). Reliability on 487 double-coded motifs: alpha 0.87 for the motif score. On 281 trickster motifs that MTurk workers had classified, the AI coder agreed with a random worker as often as workers agreed with each other (0.49 against 0.48).")
P("Result. Across 104 countries with continent fixed effects: 0.02 SD of trust per SD of the endorsement score (90 percent: -0.12 to 0.16, p = 0.82). Among 6,558 children of immigrants: 0.014 SD (90 percent: -0.005 to 0.034, p = 0.22). Political scale: -0.008 SD (p = 0.78). Language survival: OR 1.18 [0.96, 1.43] for being more endangered. The trickster measure still predicts trust: 0.28 SD across countries, 0.031 SD among immigrants (p = 0.008). Decision: inconclusive (Holm p 0.82 and 0.45).")
P("Why. The endorsement score and the trickster measure correlate only 0.30 across countries, so they capture different things. Whether a story rewards the cheat matters for trust; how much cooperation a society's motifs reward, summed over seven behaviours, does not show up.")

H3("H10: are the stories themselves selected?")
P("Result. 280 motifs reward cooperation on balance. They are carried by slightly fewer language families (IRR 0.90 [0.81, 1.01]) and continents (0.93 [0.87, 0.99]) than other motifs, and on the dated trees they are not lost more slowly (pooled coefficient on log loss rate -0.19, SE 0.20, p = 0.33). Decision: inconclusive. Breadth also reflects a motif's age and how well regions are documented.")

H3("What the retest settles")
B("The word-count problem is gone, and with it the first run's main excuse. With a human-coded, polity-level content measure, 221 polities and region fixed effects, content still does not predict how long polities last. A strong protective effect is unlikely; a modest one cannot be ruled out.")
B("How rulers behave predicts how regimes and leaders end, across countries and across leaders, and not only in hindsight. It does not survive comparing each country with itself, and it does not appear in premodern constraint codes.")
B("The cooperative behaviours are approved almost everywhere they are judged. The one formal failure, respect for property, is concentrated at the group's edge.")
B("Belief in a moralizing God is inherited along language lineages. The trees do not show it following political hierarchy.")

H3("How the next test could do better")
B("Content measured from narratives themselves, polity by polity, by coders blind to outcomes, instead of ideology codes made by historians who knew how each polity ended.")
B("More internal-collapse events: coding the remaining Seshat polities and every CrisisDB crisis, not only those that ended a polity.")
B("For H8, designs that separate conduct from stable country traits: leader successions within countries, or exogenous shocks such as failed assassinations.")
B("For H6, a new pre-registered experiment with traditional narratives and incentivized games, since the published record is thin.")

